# hdl/odocrypt/ — shared OdoCrypt hash core

This directory holds the OdoCrypt/Keccak hash core RTL shared, unmodified,
by every real board variant in this repo (`hardware/qmtech-kintex7/`,
`hardware/zynq/`, and the openXC7 build flow) — see those directories'
READMEs and wrapper source comments for how each one consumes it.

## Where it came from

`atomminer_misc.v`, `keccak800.v`, and `miner.v` are exact copies of the
same files in `exmaples/odocrypt/fpga/src/hdl/` (the stock AM01/vendor
example, originally from upstream `atomminer/am01`). They are not
epoch-dependent, so the two locations should always be identical for
these three files.

`encrypt.v` is different: OdoCrypt mutates every 10 days
(`ntime - ntime % 864000`), so this file must track the *current* epoch to
produce a working miner. The copy in `exmaples/odocrypt/` is deliberately
**left at its original upstream epoch** (`1642464000`) because it's paired
with matching test vectors in `exmaples/odocrypt/test/`. The copy here is
regenerated for whatever epoch is current.

## Why a separate copy instead of building from exmaples/ directly

`exmaples/` is meant to stay a faithful copy of the upstream vendor
example — easy to diff/merge against `upstream/master`, and safe to treat
as reference material without worrying that a real board build depends on
it byte-for-byte. Keeping the real, load-bearing build dependency in its
own location (here) makes that dependency visible instead of implicit, and
lets `encrypt.v` here diverge (by epoch) from the `exmaples/` copy without
that looking like accidental drift.

## Keeping this up to date

Regenerate `encrypt.v` with the vendored generator at `tools/odo_gen/`:

```
cd tools/odo_gen && make odo_gen
./odo_gen <current_epoch_seed> 4 encrypt_4 > ../../hdl/odocrypt/encrypt.v
```

See `tools/odo_gen/NOTICE` for how to determine the current epoch seed and
full usage notes. `keccak800.v`, `miner.v`, and `atomminer_misc.v` only
need re-copying from `exmaples/odocrypt/fpga/src/hdl/` if upstream changes
them (unlikely — they're the fixed, non-mutating part of the core).

